{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DIAGNOSIS — the premise as filed is FALSE, and the fleet-wide unblock needs NO code change. Evidence below; I have not claimed this ticket because the recorded direction (extract a seam from close_ticket) would be wrong.\n\nMAIN IS GREEN. On current main tip 07e76a1ce3: ruff reports close_ticket at complexity 17, and .github/complexity-baseline.json records ceiling 17. Consistent. `check_complexity_baseline.py --check` on a clean main checkout returns: active=60 new=0 increased=0 stale=0. At the sha in the report, 104dc4e10a, code was 19 and the recorded ceiling was ALSO 19 — likewise consistent. Neither sha has a 19-vs-17 mismatch in its own tree, so there is nothing in close_ticket to extract.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY FAILS. The CI job runs --lock, not --check. lock_against_base (scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py:455+) compares the CHANGE's baseline against MAIN TIP's, and its rules are: an entry REMOVED or a ceiling LOWERED is allowed; a ceiling RAISED or a NEW entry is a violation requiring an administrator.\n\nMy damselfly change (merged as 8f6b6c9356) extracted the signing tail from close_ticket, taking it 19 -> 17, and drained the now-stale baseline entry 19 -> 17. Lowering a ceiling is explicitly allowed, so that change passed. But it means every OPEN change whose tree still carries the pre-damselfly baseline presents ceiling 19 against main's 17 — which the lock reads as RAISING the ceiling.\n\nREPRODUCED EXACTLY. Feeding main-tip ceilings as base and the 104dc4e10a ceilings as branch into lock_against_base returns exactly one violation:\n\n  ceiling RAISED for 'src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py::close_ticket': 17 -> 19 — an administrator must override the gate to raise a ceiling\n\nThat is the reported symptom, verbatim. The same call with a rebased branch (base vs base) returns ZERO violations.\n\nIMMEDIATE UNBLOCK, NO ADMIN NEEDED: every blocked change REBASES onto current main. It does not need a new patchset beyond the rebase, and it does not need an administrator override. Any change that has not rebased since 8f6b6c9356 landed is affected; a change that never touches the baseline still carries the old FILE and so still trips it.\n\nTHE REAL DEFECT, if this ticket is kept. The lock compares against main TIP rather than the change's MERGE BASE, so it cannot distinguish 'this branch raised a ceiling' from 'main lowered it underneath an un-rebased branch'. Any future ceiling REDUCTION on main will re-break the fleet the same way — and reductions are exactly what the shrink-only ratchet is designed to encourage, so the gate currently penalises the behaviour it exists to reward. A durable fix compares the branch against merge-base(main, branch) and flags only entries the branch itself changed. Note the CI lock fetches main with --depth=1 (scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py:_fetch_base_ceilings_from_main), which is too shallow to compute a merge base, so that fetch depth has to change too.\n\nI have NOT claimed this ticket: its recorded direction is based on the false premise, and the re-scoped work (a CI-gate correctness fix) is a different change from the one described. Awaiting a decision on whether to proceed with the merge-base fix under this ticket or a new one."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786570557728076001,"uuid":"0800ff7a-149f-4502-8751-189bfdce29f3"}